      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c62a556c-6a10-40ba-a7cc-f8ef88027e85",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n**Circulate:** Teachers play a vital role in computer science education and supporting a collaborative and vibrant classroom environment. During online activities, the role of the teacher is primarily one of encouragement and support. Online lessons are meant to be student-centered, so teachers should avoid stepping in when students get stuck. Some ideas on how to do this are: \n\n - Utilize pair programming whenever possible during the activity.\n - Encourage students with questions/challenges to start by asking their partner.\n - Unanswered questions can be escalated to a nearby group, who might already know the solution.\n - Remind students to use the debugging process before you approach.\n - Have students describe the problem that they’re seeing. What is it supposed to do? What does it do? What does that tell you?\n - Remind frustrated students that frustration is a step on the path to learning, and that persistence will pay off.\n - If a student is still stuck after all of this, ask leading questions to get the student to spot an error on their own.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Discuss” slide\n\n**Discuss:** After providing students with end-of-class warnings, grab everyone's attention and get them to reflect on the experiences that they just had.\n\n - _Did anyone feel frustrated during any of the puzzles?_\n - _Did anyone notice the need to be persistent?_\n\n**Transition:** Have students take a moment to reflect on the lesson for themselves."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c62a556c-6a10-40ba-a7cc-f8ef88027e85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2232ffb2-92dd-42b6-b1cb-fb337b038282"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce392267-0d57-45b1-b804-5efc46e6d72b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Reflect” slide\n\n**Reflect:**\n_Why is it important to program your blocks in a specific order?_\n",
        "name": "Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce392267-0d57-45b1-b804-5efc46e6d72b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4d508e5e-e72f-450e-bb72-e1a8c6bd635f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9aedf77d-941d-44df-8f69-964a27f6da65",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Use these activities to enhance student learning. They can be used as outside of class activities or other enrichment.\r\n\r\n**Create Your Own**\r\n\r\nIn small groups, let students design their own mazes and challenge each other to write programs to solve them. For added fun, make life-size mazes with students as the pig and bird."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9aedf77d-941d-44df-8f69-964a27f6da65",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ef9cb48-407f-4ba4-87b3-6ca0232dbea8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dd3439b-b2e5-495b-a48b-7213cbdb9d64",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "###Bugs, Bugs & More Bugs (45-60 minutes)\n\n![](https://curriculum.code.org/media/uploads/elaSmall.png) ![](https://curriculum.code.org/media/uploads/mathSmall.png) **Computer Science + English Language Arts + Math**\n\n[Bugs, Bugs & More Bugs](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1TdvVXM9Tf8lv9L3rgDqMJFTe-iG0PjTpMtBb2f4pXDo/edit#)  is an optional activity aligned to Common Core ELA and Math standards, written by our teacher community. Students will practice debugging as they fix code, correct a math problem and proofread writing samples.\n\n*Standards Addressed*:\n- **CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.2.1.B:** Form and use frequently occurring irregular plural nouns (e.g., feet, children, teeth, mice, fish).\n\n- **CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.2.2.A:** Capitalize holidays, product names, and geographic names.\n\n- **CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.2.2.C:** Use an apostrophe to form contractions and frequently occurring possessives.\n\n- **CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.2.OA.B.2:** Fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies.\n\n- **CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.2.NBT.A.4:** Compare two three-digit numbers based on meanings of the hundreds, tens, and ones digits, using >, =, and < symbols to record the results of comparisons.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dd3439b-b2e5-495b-a48b-7213cbdb9d64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0128db-807a-4985-aec5-fe16b94e7d4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8edc3da2-82bd-449b-8df1-b479cef234aa",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Reflect” slide\n\nAsk students to think about problems they have to solve in everyday life.\n\n**Reflect:**\n - _How do you fix something in your everyday life that isn't working?_\n - _Do you follow a specific series of steps?_\n",
        "name": "Reflect"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8edc3da2-82bd-449b-8df1-b479cef234aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "075c94ea-088a-4d09-b38a-bb9ea17a1919",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Vocabulary” slide\n\nThis lesson has three new and important vocabulary words:\n\n - **Bug** - Part of a program that does not work correctly.\n\n - **Debugging** - Finding and fixing problems in an algorithm or program.\n\n - **Persistence** - Not giving up. \n\nPersistence works best when you try things many different ways, many different times.\n\n\n\n",
        "name": "Vocabulary"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "075c94ea-088a-4d09-b38a-bb9ea17a1919",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b80a511-63b6-4e32-ae6c-16e9303426b3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Debugging in Maze” slide\n\n \n\n",
        "name": "Introduction"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b80a511-63b6-4e32-ae6c-16e9303426b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b87df2d-1412-4a7c-bbcb-e052eef1d2ae",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\nThe puzzles in this unit have already been solved for you (yay!), but they don't seem to be working (boo!)\nWe call the problems in these programs \"bugs,\" and it will be your job to \"debug\" them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b87df2d-1412-4a7c-bbcb-e052eef1d2ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0028fdda-22fb-4692-9c27-c37606c26afc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": " \n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Debugging” slide\n\n\n**Say:** _Do you see an error in your program?_\n-  _Work through the program step by step to find the error._ \n- _Try the first step, did it work?_ \n- _Then the second, how about now?_"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0028fdda-22fb-4692-9c27-c37606c26afc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "044cf86c-2385-44b6-82d8-3b0d11ffe957",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": " If you make sure that everything is working line by line, then when you get to the place that your code isn't doing what it's supposed to, you know that you've found a bug. Once you've discovered your bug, you can work to fix (or \"debug\") it!\n\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "044cf86c-2385-44b6-82d8-3b0d11ffe957",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42db2bc2-8c56-49d4-a7d4-6d4c80f1155b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If you think it will build excitement in the class you can introduce the character of today's puzzles, *Scrat* from _\"Ice Age\"_. If students aren't familiar with *Scrat*, show some video clips of the quirky squirrel running into trouble."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42db2bc2-8c56-49d4-a7d4-6d4c80f1155b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11f44176-5ea3-4233-8ee1-58defaf00f58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f7db599-8a91-4373-add7-d5a5e2846c58",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before letting the students start on the computer, remind them of the advantages of pair programming and asking their peers for help. Sit students in pairs and recommend they ask at least two peers for help before they come to a teacher.",
        "name": "Debugging in Maze"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f7db599-8a91-4373-add7-d5a5e2846c58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9dc16cf4-b518-4feb-b085-fddd2b8d8479"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1013b89b-dc20-473c-9737-93566c8ccd23",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Debugging with the Step Button” video",
        "progression_name": "Video:  Debugging with the Step Button"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1013b89b-dc20-473c-9737-93566c8ccd23",
